            "text": "{\"study_topic_id\":\"study-bf7dd3528cd80a208588\",\"overview\":{\"text\":\"Woodcut, also called woodblock printing, is a relief-printing method: a printmaker cuts away parts of a wooden block, inks the raised surface, and presses paper against it. The carved-away spaces stay unprinted, and the transferred image appears reversed. Because a block can make repeated impressions, woodcuts helped reproduce pictures and texts; the National Gallery of Art dates the technique in China to the seventh century. In Japanese ukiyo-e, woodblock printing became a sophisticated team craft involving a designer, block cutter, printer, and publisher. Color prints used separate, carefully aligned blocks for different hues, producing layered images such as Hokusai’s famous Great Wave.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nga.gov/educational-resources/printmaking-basics\",\"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/japanese-woodblock-prints-ukiyo-e\",\"https://wwws.loc.gov/preservation/conservators/japanesepillar/index.html\"]},\"key_facts\":[{\"text\":\"Woodcut is a relief process: ink prints from the block’s raised areas, while carved recesses remain blank.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nga.gov/educational-resources/printmaking-basics\",\"https://wwws.loc.gov/preservation/conservators/japanesepillar/index.html\"]},{\"text\":\"A print transfers the block’s design in reverse; repeating the process makes multiple impressions.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.nga.gov/educational-resources/printmaking-basics\",\"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/japanese-woodblock-prints-ukiyo-e\"]},{\"text\":\"Traditional Japanese ukiyo-e production involved a publisher, designer, block cutter, and printer.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/japanese-woodblock-prints-ukiyo-e\"]},{\"text\":\"For Japanese multicolor prints, separate blocks were carved for different colors and aligned with registration guides.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/japanese-woodblock-prints-ukiyo-e\",\"https://wwws.loc.gov/preservation/conservators/japanesepillar/index.html\"]},{\"text\":\"Woodblock prints helped circulate images to broad audiences; ukiyo-e depicted subjects including actors, landscapes, and everyday life.\",\"source_urls\":[\"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/japanese-woodblock-prints-ukiyo-e\"]}],\"sources\":[{\"title\":\"Printmaking Basics\",\"url\":\"https://www.nga.gov/educational-resources/printmaking-basics\",\"publisher\":\"National Gallery of Art\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:34:15.542333+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Defines woodcut as relief printing and describes the carved-away areas, raised inked surface, and seventh-century Chinese history.\"},{\"title\":\"Japanese woodblock prints (ukiyo-e)\",\"url\":\"https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/japanese-woodblock-prints-ukiyo-e\",\"publisher\":\"Victoria and Albert Museum\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:34:15.542333+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Supports ukiyo-e’s popular subjects and wide circulation, the four-person production team, and the use of separate aligned blocks for color.\"},{\"title\":\"Conservation Treatment of a Japanese Pillar Print\",\"url\":\"https://wwws.loc.gov/preservation/conservators/japanesepillar/index.html\",\"publisher\":\"Library of Congress\",\"retrieved_at\":\"2026-09-24T18:34:15.542333+00:00\",\"supports\":\"Explains relief printing, pressing paper onto an inked block, and Japanese keyblocks, separate color blocks, and registration guides.\"}],\"research_queries\":[\"site:metmuseum.org woodcut relief printmaking woodblock print process\",\"site:nga.gov woodcut printmaking relief woodblock educational\",\"site:britishmuseum.org Japanese woodblock printing ukiyo-e process woodblock\",\"site:vam.ac.uk woodblock printing ukiyo-e process woodblock prints\",\"site:loc.gov woodblock printing Japanese ukiyo-e process\"],\"notes\":\"Used directly opened pages from the National Gallery of Art, Victoria and Albert Museum, and Library of Congress. Overview word count: 103. Source pages support the process, historical context, Japanese production methods, and cultural significance stated above.\",\"unresolved_concerns\":[],\"self_check_complete\":true}"
