      "text": "Woodcut, also called woodblock printing, is a relief-printing method: a printmaker cuts away parts of a wooden block, inks the raised surface, and presses paper against it. The carved-away spaces stay unprinted, and the transferred image appears reversed. Because a block can make repeated impressions, woodcuts helped reproduce pictures and texts; the National Gallery of Art dates the technique in China to the seventh century. In Japanese ukiyo-e, woodblock printing became a sophisticated team craft involving a designer, block cutter, printer, and publisher. Color prints used separate, carefully aligned blocks for different hues, producing layered images such as Hokusai’s famous Great Wave.",
      "source_urls": [
        "https://www.nga.gov/educational-resources/printmaking-basics",
        "https://www.vam.ac.uk/articles/japanese-woodblock-prints-ukiyo-e",
